<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <Import Project="../UnityReferenceAssemblies.props" />
  <!--
    A LOCAL TYPE-CHECKER for the package's THREE DEPENDENCY-INJECTION INTEGRATION assemblies, and
    the FIFTH source tree this repository ships. (`Samples~/DI - */Scripts/**` binds the same three
    containers and is still gated only by the .unitypackage export smoke test; see below.)

    Why this exists (#687). Four check projects existed and between them they missed one tree.
    `TypeCheck.csproj` names `$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Integrations/**/*.cs` in its `Exclude`, and
    EditorCheck, TestCheck and EditorTestCheck inherit that exclusion verbatim, so the 19 files
    under `Runtime/Integrations/` - the Reflex, VContainer and Zenject relational-component bridges -
    were compiled by CI and by nothing local. No `WUH###` rule and no `WPROTO###` rule had ever run
    over them.

    It has been paid for. FOUR of those files carried the same rule-7 violation until #644's audit:

        AttributeMetadataCache cache = _metadataCache ?? AttributeMetadataCache.Instance;
        if (cache == null) { return; }

    `AttributeMetadataCache` is a `ScriptableObject`, `??` is a CLR-null test, and a DESTROYED
    injected instance is therefore passed straight through rather than replaced - whereupon the
    Unity `==` on the next line reads it as null and returns. VContainer's
    `RelationalSceneLoadListener` and Zenject's `RelationalComponentSceneInitializer` bailed there:
    every relational field in that scene stayed null, with no log. `WUH003` reports exactly that
    shape, and it never saw the file. The four sites are fixed; this project is the reason they
    could exist.

    ==================== WHY A FIFTH PROJECT AND NOT A WIDER FOURTH ====================

    A new define configuration of TypeCheck or EditorCheck was the cheaper option and is the wrong
    one, for three reasons that are all about what the OTHER gates would then be compiling:

      1. The shims declare the top-level namespaces `Reflex`, `VContainer` and `Zenject`. Carried by
         TypeCheck they would be compiled into every one of its four configurations, widening the
         surface of the gate that judges `Runtime/**` with types nothing in that tree binds. Carried
         CONDITIONALLY they would add a fifth and sixth configuration to a project that already
         builds four times.
      2. `Runtime/Integrations/**` is not part of `WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers`. It is three
         separate asmdefs that REFERENCE it, so the runtime arrives as an assembly rather than as
         sources, and the analyzers then report over this tree ALONE. A wider TypeCheck would fold
         all of it into one compilation and re-report every `Runtime/**` finding here, which for a
         gate whose whole subject is "no `WUH###` rule has ever run over these 19 files" is exactly
         the signal to keep clean. The `<ProjectReference>` below is DocSamplesCheck's shape rather
         than a new one.
      3. `scripts/lint-typecheck-asmdef-references.js` discovers a gate by being a
         `Generator~/*Check/` directory and `scripts/typecheck-controls.js` keys its negative
         controls on a PROJECT with an anchor from "the tree that project is the only local compiler
         of". A configuration is invisible to the first and unnameable by the second, so the tree
         would be compiled without being GATED - which is the distinction this issue is about.

    ======================= WHAT IT COMPILES, AND IN WHICH BRANCHES =======================

    All 19 files under `Runtime/Integrations/`, with no exclusions, against
    `Shims/{Reflex,VContainer,Zenject}Shim.cs` and the runtime's built assembly. It loads BOTH
    shipped analyzer DLLs, as every other check project does, so `WPROTO###` and `WUH###` fire here
    exactly as they do in the editor. It is a type-checker, not a test runner: the real containers
    are exercised by the Unity integration legs, which install the pinned packages from
    `.github/integration-packages.json`.

    The tree's own conditional compilation is exactly FIVE symbols, and this project builds every
    branch of them:

      * `REFLEX_PRESENT`, `VCONTAINER_PRESENT`, `ZENJECT_PRESENT` - the three asmdefs'
        `defineConstraints`. Off, the file is empty; there is no second branch to compile, so all
        three are always on here.
      * `REFLEX_14_0_OR_NEWER` - Reflex SPLIT its registration API at 14.0.0 (`AddSingleton` became
        `RegisterType`/`RegisterValue` plus the `Lifetime`/`Resolution` enums) and
        `RelationalComponentsInstaller` carries both. Both ship, so both compile:
        `typecheck:integrations:default` and `typecheck:integrations:legacy-reflex`.
      * `UNITY_EDITOR` - five of the files carry an editor-only safety net that schedules a second
        assignment pass through `EditorApplication.delayCall`. That branch ships in the editor and
        the code AROUND it ships in a player, so both compile: `typecheck:integrations:default`
        defines it and holds the `UnityEditor` reference, `typecheck:integrations:player` neither.

    Odin, `WALLSTOP_PROTO` and `SINGLE_THREADED` are deliberately NOT configurations here. No file
    under `Runtime/Integrations/` mentions any of them, and all three change declarations in
    `Runtime/**`, which TypeCheck already builds four ways. Adding them would re-judge that tree
    through a reference rather than cover anything new in this one.

    ============================ WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER ============================

      1. Whether the shims MATCH the real packages. A shim is a claim about someone else's API, and
         nothing local can check it - the same limitation `ShimsOdin/OdinEditorShim.cs` has carried
         since #347. It is bounded by being minimal: only what these 19 files name is declared, so a
         wrong declaration can only be wrong about a member the package really does call, and the
         Unity integration legs call it against the real package.
      2. Behaviour, and IL2CPP. A green build here is necessary and NOT sufficient.
      3. `Samples~/DI - */Scripts/**`, which bind the same three containers. Those compile as a
         release payload through the `.unitypackage` export smoke gate, not here.

    Like the other check projects, this lives under `Generator~`, which Unity ignores by the
    trailing-tilde convention, and ships to nobody.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.1</TargetFramework>
    <!-- Unity 2021.3, the oldest version in the CI matrix, is the constraint that matters. -->
    <LangVersion>9</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <EnableDefaultCompileItems>false</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
    <!--
      THE ASSEMBLY NAMES ITSELF AFTER ONE OF THE THREE TREES IT COMPILES, and that is deliberate.
      `Runtime/Core/Extension/UnityObjectExtensions.cs` is `internal`, and `Runtime/AssemblyInfo.cs`
      grants `InternalsVisibleTo` to all three integration assemblies by name precisely so they can
      call its `FindObjectsByType` version shim, at 11 sites across 5 of these files. An assembly named
      anything else fails with eleven x CS0122 - measured, and it is what this project answered on
      its first build. The three grants are IDENTICAL, so borrowing the first of them alphabetically
      gives this compilation exactly the access Unity gives each integration and not one member
      more. Same mechanism, and same reason, as TestCheck and EditorTestCheck calling themselves
      `WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers`; EditorTestCheck's header records it as the blocker it is.
    -->
    <AssemblyName>WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Integration.Reflex</AssemblyName>
    <!-- WUH010 is the one WUH### rule that ships off; the package holds itself to it here, on the
         same terms as the other four gates. See the comment in the ruleset itself. -->
    <CodeAnalysisRuleSet>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../CheckProjects.ruleset</CodeAnalysisRuleSet>
    <RepoRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)/../..'))</RepoRoot>
    <!-- Shared with EditorCheck, and pinned there for the reasons its header gives. Only the
         editor configuration resolves it. -->
    <UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion>2021.1.14.1</UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>false</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <!--
      XML doc validation, the switch TypeCheck turned on for `Runtime/**` and EditorCheck for
      `Editor/**`, pointed at the third tree the package ships as SOURCE. Roslyn checks doc comments
      only when asked to emit the file, and Unity never asks, so a `see cref` naming a member that
      does not exist compiled clean here forever. Two are silenced rather than fixed, exactly as in
      the other gates, because neither is a defect: CS1591 is "no doc comment on a public member",
      a different rule, and CS1573 fires on every partially documented method.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0414;CS0649;CS0219;CS0618;CS0612;CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
    <!--
      CS0067, exactly one site and a shim artifact rather than a judgment about the sources:
      `Reflex.Core.ContainerBuilder.OnContainerBuilt` is an event the package SUBSCRIBES to and a
      type-check shim never raises. Raising it from a stub method to satisfy the compiler would put
      a lie in the shim; naming the diagnostic here says what is going on.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS0067</NoWarn>
    <!-- NU1701: `Unity3D.SDK` publishes a bare `lib/` folder with no target framework, so restoring
         it into a netstandard2.1 project is a framework mismatch by construction. Scoped to that
         one PackageReference as well as here. CS1701: its `UnityEditor.dll` names
         `UnityEngine 0.0.0.0` and binds to the `UnityEngine.Modules` type-forwarding facade. Both
         are EditorCheck's, for EditorCheck's reasons. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1701;NU1701</NoWarn>
    <!-- Same split as the other gates: EPS01 is at zero and is escalated, the rest of the family is
         silenced because measured most of it is wrong about Span. Inventory on #517. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);EPS02;EPS03;EPS04;EPS05;EPS06;EPS07;EPS08;EPS09;EPS10;EPS11;EPS12</NoWarn>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);EPS01</WarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- The three asmdefs' defineConstraints. Off, every file in the tree is an empty namespace, so
         there is no second branch and these are not configurations. -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);REFLEX_PRESENT;VCONTAINER_PRESENT;ZENJECT_PRESENT</DefineConstants>
    <!-- Reflex's registration API split at 14.0.0. The asmdef sets this versionDefine for a UPM
         Reflex >= 14.0.0; CI pins 14.3.1, and a vendored 13.x still resolves the other branch. -->
    <WallstopReflex14 Condition="'$(WallstopReflex14)' == ''">true</WallstopReflex14>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(WallstopReflex14)' == 'true'"
      >$(DefineConstants);REFLEX_14_0_OR_NEWER</DefineConstants>
    <!--
      UNITY_EDITOR plus the symbol set a real Unity 2021.3 editor defines, exactly as EditorCheck
      spells them. The integration asmdefs set no `includePlatforms`, so Unity compiles this tree
      BOTH ways: with these in the editor and without them in a player. `:player` is that second
      half, and it drops the `UnityEditor` reference with them so a `UnityEditor` type used outside
      an `#if UNITY_EDITOR` fails HERE rather than in a standalone build.
    -->
    <WallstopUnityEditor Condition="'$(WallstopUnityEditor)' == ''">true</WallstopUnityEditor>
    <UnityVersionDefines>UNITY_2021;UNITY_2021_3;UNITY_2021_3_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2021_2_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2021_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_3_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_2_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2020_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2019_4_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2019_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_2018_1_OR_NEWER;UNITY_5_3_OR_NEWER</UnityVersionDefines>
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);$(UnityVersionDefines)</DefineConstants>
    <DefineConstants Condition="'$(WallstopUnityEditor)' == 'true'"
      >$(DefineConstants);UNITY_EDITOR</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Analyzer only: it ships to nobody and contributes no runtime assets. -->
    <PackageReference Include="ErrorProne.NET.Structs" Version="0.1.2">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>analyzers</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <!-- The analyzer Unity actually loads, so a contract the generator refuses fails HERE. It is
         also a source generator the compilation cannot resolve `[WProtoContract]` without. -->
    <Analyzer Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Analyzers/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Proto.Generator.dll" />
    <!-- The general-purpose WUH### analyzers ship in the same folder and Unity loads both, so both
         have to be loaded here or a local gate covers less than the editor does. This one is the
         whole point of #687: WUH003 is the diagnostic that never saw this tree. -->
    <Analyzer Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Analyzers/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.Analyzers.dll" />
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="WProtoDisableModuleInitializer" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The package's runtime API surface, built once by TypeCheck rather than recompiled here. That is
      DocSamplesCheck's shape, and it buys two things. It matches Unity, where the three integration
      asmdefs REFERENCE `WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers` rather than being part of it. And it keeps the
      analyzers pointed at this tree ALONE: recompiling `Runtime/**` here, the way EditorCheck
      legitimately does for `Editor/**`, would re-report every `Runtime/**` finding through a gate
      whose entire subject is 19 files no analyzer had ever seen. TypeCheck remains the gate that
      JUDGES that tree; a divergence here is a bug in one of the two.

      What it does NOT buy is an `internal` boundary. `Runtime/AssemblyInfo.cs` grants all three
      integration assemblies `InternalsVisibleTo`, so their `internal` access is real and this
      project has to reproduce it - see the AssemblyName comment above.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck.csproj">
      <!-- The negative-control property is this project's alone. Left to flow, it would inject the
           control file into the referenced build as well and report a diagnostic from the wrong
           compilation. -->
      <UndefineProperties>WallstopCheckControl</UndefineProperties>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(WallstopUnityEditor)' == 'true'">
    <!-- ExcludeAssets="all" because only lib/UnityEditor.dll is wanted; the package's own
         UnityEngine.dll must NOT be referenced alongside the UnityEngine.Modules facade.
         GeneratePathProperty is what makes $(PkgUnity3D_SDK) resolvable below. -->
    <PackageReference
      Include="Unity3D.SDK"
      Version="$(UnityEditorReferenceAssemblyVersion)"
      GeneratePathProperty="true"
      ExcludeAssets="all"
      NoWarn="NU1701"
    />
    <!-- The editor half. Private=false: nothing is deployed, this project only type-checks. -->
    <Reference Include="UnityEditor">
      <HintPath>$(PkgUnity3D_SDK)/lib/UnityEditor.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    NO VENDORED BINARY IS REFERENCED HERE, and that is measured rather than assumed. `protobuf-net`
    and `protobuf-net.Core` were on this list first, copied from the projects that compile
    `Runtime/**` as SOURCES; dropping both compiled clean in all three configurations. `Runtime/**`
    arrives as an assembly through the ProjectReference above, and nothing under
    `Runtime/Integrations/**` binds a type out of a vendored plugin, so the whole list was dead
    weight. Holding a reference nothing needs only widens the gap between this gate and what Unity
    compiles, which is #598's lesson and the reason `System.IO.Pipelines` is gone from TypeCheck.
    This is also why `scripts/tests/test-lint-typecheck-asmdef-references.js` records this project
    as the one gate with no bundled reference to parse.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Unity's BCL exposes ModuleInitializerAttribute and the netstandard2.1 reference assemblies
         do not, so the generator's registrar needs TypeCheck's polyfill here too. It is `internal`,
         which is why it is compiled rather than inherited through the reference. -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)/Generator~/WallstopStudios.UnityHelpers.TypeCheck/Shims/ModuleInitializerShim.cs" />
    <!-- The three DI containers, each declaring only what these 19 files name. -->
    <Compile Include="Shims/*.cs" />
    <!-- The three integration asmdefs' scope, in full. There are no exclusions and there should
         never be one: a file dropped from here is a file back in the hole #687 is about. -->
    <Compile Include="$(RepoRoot)/Runtime/Integrations/**/*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Target Name="AnnounceIntegrationConfiguration" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <Message
      Importance="High"
      Text="[typecheck:integrations] Runtime/Integrations/** against SHIMS, not the real containers: Reflex 14.3.1, VContainer 1.18.0, Extenject 9.2.0-stcf3 are the packages CI installs and none is on nuget.org. REFLEX_14_0_OR_NEWER=$(WallstopReflex14), UNITY_EDITOR=$(WallstopUnityEditor). A shim is a claim about someone else's API; the Unity integration legs are what check it. See this project's header and issue #687."
    />
  </Target>
  <!--
    A NEGATIVE CONTROL hook, set by `npm run typecheck:controls` and by nothing else.

    This gate reports a defect by failing. A gate that has stopped seeing its sources, lost an
    analyzer, or been silenced by configuration reports exactly what a clean tree reports, so a
    green build here is only evidence once the same build has been shown to go red on a defect it
    is supposed to catch (#636). The control script injects one throwaway file carrying a known
    diagnostic and asserts the build names it.

    An empty property adds no file, so an ordinary build is byte-identical to one without this.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(WallstopCheckControl)' != ''">
    <Compile Include="$(WallstopCheckControl)" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
